            <RURComments xml:space="preserve">
                <airSeal>

&lt;b&gt;Observed Air Leakage:&lt;/b&gt;

Overall, your house has a moderate amount of excess air leakage, however there are some key areas where air leakage occurs. I did note some air leakage at some locations in your basement, primarily around your wood stove and windows.



I noted that neither of your bathroom fans appears to be functioning well. I recommend you replace both your bathroom exhaust fans with an ENERGY STARÂ® rated model.



I did not see a combustion air supply in your home. Combustion air supply provides fresh air for your furnace and wood stoves. I recommend that if you do not have a combustion air supply, you have one installed prior to doing any other air sealing work. 



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

For a minimum 10% improvement you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $130&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;5.2 GJ&lt;/b&gt;, and qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$500 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, you must have a home energy evaluation, which includes an air leakage test, before and after your retrofits. For a full rebate, the rebate application, with a copy of the first page of both home energy evaluation reports, must be received within &lt;u&gt;three months&lt;/u&gt; of the second energy evaluation. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;



&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;

</airSeal>
                <mainWalls>

The exterior walls of your house appear to be 2x4 studs with 4 inches (R12) of fiberglass batt insulation, as well as 1.5 inches (R6) of rigid styrofoam insulation. I have modelled adding an additional R15 to bring the R-value in your walls to approximately R32.



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By adding R15 to your current walls, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $280&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;10.9 GJ&lt;/b&gt;, and qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$1,875 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total exterior wall area and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 0.7 (R-3.8). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. You must apply and receive pre-approval for an EEIP insulation rebate.



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website:

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;



&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;



</mainWalls>
                <exposedFloors>

The exposed floors of your home appear to be 2x10 joists with 10 inches (R32) of fiberglass batt insulation. I have modelled adding 3 inches (R15) of rigid extruded polystyrene insulation. 



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By adding 3 inches (R15) of rigid foam extruded polystyrene insulation, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $8.00&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;0.3 GJ&lt;/b&gt;, and qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$190 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, you must insulate your entire exposed floor and a minimum floor area of 14 square metres (150 square feet) must be insulated. You must also increase the insulation value by a minimum of RSI 3.5 (R-20). You must apply and receive pre-approval for an EEIP insulation rebate.



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;



&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;

</exposedFloors>
                <foundation>

Your basement walls appear to be 8 inches of concrete with 2x4 framing with 4 inches (R12) of fiberglass batt insulation over 90% of the walls, and the remaining 10% is uninsulated. I have modeled insulating the entire basement to R24, which could be done in a number of different ways.





&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By insulating your entire basement to R24, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $1,460&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;56.7 GJ&lt;/b&gt;, and qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$1,250 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total foundation wall area including crawlspaces (when applicable), and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 1.8 (R-10). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. You must apply and receive pre-approval for an EEIP insulation rebate.



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;



&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;

</foundation>
                <windows>

As all of your windows are older double pane ones that do not appear to have a Low E coating or an insulating gas fill between the panes of glass, I have modeled the potential energy savings associated with upgrading all of them with ENERGY STARÂ® qualified windows rated for climate zone 3. In addition, I recommend triple paned, double low-E, argon gas filled, hinged windows. 



I have not modelled the energy savings associated with replacing your doors, but should you need to replace them for any reason, I recommend ENERGY STARÂ® qualified patio door rated for climate zone 3.



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt; 

By replacing all your windows with triple paned, double low-E, argon gas filled, hinged, ENERGY STARÂ® qualified windows rated for climate zone 3, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $440&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, and decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;17.2GJ&lt;/b&gt;. There are currently no rebates available for installing new windows from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;

</windows>
                <HotWaterSystem>

You currently have a 270 litre electric hot water tank. Although electric hot water tanks are efficient from the perspective that 100% of the electricity used either heats the water inside or the heat radiating from the tank supplements the heating of the home, electricity is very costly. 



Since your electric hot water tank is fairly new, and in good condition, I would recommend adding an R5 insulation blanket to your hot water tank. 



Another option is to replace your electric hot water tank with a propane fired instantaneous hot water heater, with an efficiency factor of 0.92 or greater. 



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By adding an R5 insulation blanket to your electric hot water heater, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $100&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, and decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;0.1 GJ&lt;/b&gt;. There are currently no rebates available for this measure from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



By replacing your electric hot water heater r, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $865&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, and increase your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;0.6 GJ&lt;/b&gt;, and be eligible for a rebate of &lt;b&gt;$1,250&lt;/b&gt; through the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;

</HotWaterSystem>
                <HeatingSystem>

Your current oil fired furnace was originally rated at 83% AFUE. When it is time to replace it, I have modeled an upgrade to an ENERGY STARÂ® qualified high-efficiency oil furnace with a minimum efficiency of 87% AFUE. If you choose a high-efficiency oil furnace (direct vented out a side wall), the current chimney can be eliminated altogether.



Another option is to switch from oil to propane, and install a direct vent, condensing propane fired furnace with an efficiency of 0.95 or higher. 



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By replacing your heating system with a sealed combustion oil-fired furnace with an efficiency of 87% AFUE, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $340&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;13.4 GJ&lt;/b&gt;, and qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$750 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



By replacing your heating system with a sealed combustion propane-fired furnace with an efficiency of 95% AFUE, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $1,465&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;39.1 GJ&lt;/b&gt;, and qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$750 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within &lt;u&gt;three months&lt;/u&gt; of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;



&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;

</HeatingSystem>
                <WaterConservation>

I recommend you replace both toilets in your home. When replacing your toilets, purchase 4.8 Liters or less, or dual-flush toilets. 



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt; 

By replacing your toilet(s) with a new 4.8 Liters or less, or dual-flush toilet(s), you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save ~$50 &lt;/b&gt; per person, per year in water and energy savings.  There are currently no rebates available for toilets from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;



</WaterConservation>
